{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Also today my complaint is bitter</b> — 'bitter' indicates that my complaint is beyond all complaints, as in 'the poor of Israel' [meaning] very poor and his condition is unusual." "<b>My hand</b> — 'my stroke,' as in 'my hand of night is poured out' (and do not compare [this root with that one]). The sense is that my stroke is heavier than my sigh."], :book :job, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {1 "The quoted phrase 'ידי לילה נגרה' does not correspond to any biblical verse; it may be a paraphrastic fusion or a textual corruption. Translated hyper-literally. The word ולא תדמה is read as 'and do not compare,' i.e., do not assimilate this root to a similar one."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Job -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/eyov/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:03:29.609906049Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}